leadResults = [
 {"leadId":"L-645","outcome":"cleared","answer":"bor v2.11.1 cannot pair one bytecode's JUMPDEST bitmap with another code hash. V2 workers read code through ParallelStateDB, whose readCodeKey caches the first CodePath read of each address for the whole incarnation, explicitly so that resolveCode and resolveCodeHash follow one delegation target (core/state/parallel_statedb.go lines 359-381). The hash it returns is keccak256 of the bytes read, or the parent trie hash of parent-state bytes. A redelegating attacker can at most leave behind a bitmap from a speculative incarnation, and that bitmap is the correct one for the bytes whose hash keys it. The prefetcher, the serial processor, the witness path and the producer use single-threaded StateDBs or per-EVM maps. No transaction ordering and no producer role changes this. The 29 push-data 0x5b bytes of the PoolManager are not named by any push constant, so even a wrong bitmap could not redirect a jump."},
 {"leadId":"L-646","outcome":"cleared","answer":"BSC v1.8.2 adds a process-wide 2,048 entry LRU of bitmaps keyed by code hash, shared by import, prefetch, mining and RPC. Every key is the hash of the bytes analysed because code hash and code come from one serial StateDB and every SetCode path computes the hash from the bytes, including RPC overrides. reth-bsc v0.1.4 keeps the jump table inside the revm Bytecode value. avalanchego 1.15.3 with libevm keeps a per-message map and has no EIP-7702."},
 {"leadId":"L-647","outcome":"cleared","answer":"Monad v0.16.5 and v0.16.7 always fetch code by the code hash they read, from stores whose keys are keccak256 of the stored bytes, so interpreter code and native code under a hash are always those of the bytes with that hash. Optimistic execution re-runs a transaction whose recorded code_hash changed (can_merge)."},
 {"leadId":"L-651","outcome":"inconclusive","answer":"The v2.11.1 release notes say that v2.11.1 is the public release of v2.11.1-priv, which most validators already ran, and that operators on v2.11.1-priv run the same code (https://github.com/0xPolygon/bor/releases/tag/v2.11.1). polygon-bor-rpc.publicnode.com now answers bor/ebcfea01452d/v2.11.1-private, a third commit after cb3d19c5f690 and f2a2d63e69f6. None of the three commits can be fetched from raw.githubusercontent.com (404 for the short ids), so the core/vm, core/blockstm and core/state of the private builds were not compared with the tag. The equivalence rests on Polygon's statement."},
]
leads = [
 {"chainIds":[143],"lens":"L1","question":"Monad v0.16.7 removed the YP K.1 ripemd touch from execute_message.cpp with a comment that reverted state depends on whether the bytecode ran as compiled native code or in the interpreter. Which other reverted state, if any, differs between the two paths in v0.16.5 and v0.16.7 (transient storage, warm sets, touched accounts), and can a reverted inner frame of the PoolManager leave a different TSTORE or touched set on a validator that compiled it than on one that interpreted it?","why":"The PoolManager's lock and deltas live in transient storage and are rolled back on revert. A path-dependent revert would let validators disagree, and if a reverted TSTORE survived on one path, an unlock flag or delta could leak within a transaction.","priority":"medium"},
 {"chainIds":[137],"lens":"L1","question":"bor v2.12.0-beta adds --precompile-cache (#2431), which gives the serial import path and the block builder the per-block shared VM caches. When v2.12.0 ships, does the builder's shared JUMPDEST cache still receive code and code hash from one single-threaded StateDB, and is the codeReadCache logic unchanged?","why":"It would extend the cache that V2 and the prefetcher share to the producer, so any pairing fault there would reach the canonical block.","priority":"low"},
 {"chainIds":[4326,999],"lens":"L1","question":"The MegaETH sequencer's compiling executor and the HyperEVM validator are closed. Do they cache analysis or compiled code keyed by a code hash read apart from the code?","why":"These are the only producers on the 21 networks whose code caches cannot be read, and both write state that public clients do not re-execute in full.","priority":"low"},
]
